A||||4||gamman||clutch questions||||5||
Z||000000||gamman||09-28-2000||12:50 AM||||I have 4 multiple choice questions:<P>1. Do you have or ever have clutch problems on what year of car?<P>2.What was your original symptom?<BR>hard getting in gear while stationary<BR>hard getting in gear while moving or warm<BR>bad peddle placement ie: off the floor etc.<BR>other: <P>3.What did you do to fix the clutch problems?<BR>fluids<BR>rebuilt:<BR>replaced:<P>4.On a percentage basis, where did the car start off, and where did it end up?<P><BR>I DO NOT want to hear why each is better, or why the viscocity of peanut butter will or won't work in the engine, I don't care. I want to get a set of numbers as to which solution has the most people using it. If enough people respond, we can even post averages for each unique solution to this problem, or maybe it is not a problem.<P>Sorry for the hand holding, sometimes necessary.<P>Gerry.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Wade87GT||09-30-2000||12:05 AM||||I had a clutch engagement problem a few years ago. The (soon-to-be-ex) wife help me bleed the system. She worked the peddle and actually bent the rod the goes to the master cylinder in the process! And she's just a little thing (5'3" 110 lbs)! Anyway, changed the M/C with a Pontiac unit, bled the system with no problems. Two weeks ago, leaking M/C, replaced with Wagner unit. It sucks air somehow, initially it bleed out a lot of air using a vacuum. If I actuate the clutch pedal during the bleed process it will suck ALOT of air. Can't find any evidence of a leak anywhere. No internal leakage since the slave cylinder rod does not retract when holding the clutch pedal for a long time (the right length stick wedged in correctly). A good bleed lasts about 3 days. Funny thing, I tried bleeding according the "Manual". Depress pedal, open bleed screw and then close screw after air passes. Then the pedal will not retract! As if a vacuum is formed within the system. Bad new M/C? Replacement M/C should arrive tomorrow. P.S. I read the entire "Clutch Thread", all 5 pages and still couldn't find an answer. But I'll let you know how mine turns out. Thirteen years later and we're still doing trial and error.||||reg||9
Z||000002||Rodrv6||09-30-2000||11:24 AM||||The only clutch problem I've had on my 84 occured when the car was about 8 months old. The slave cylinder seized in the extended position during a gear change.  The factory had misaligned it during installation and the pushrod was rubbing on the side of the piston bore.  It scored it up bad enough to cause the seizure.  I've had no other problems except for a couple of replacement clutch discs over the years.  16 years and 330,000 miles later I'm still using the original master cylinder and ALUMINUM pedal. I do replace the fluid every couple of years and have had no problem bleeding it using the normal "pedal push" method.  Maybe I'm just lucky <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||09-30-2000||01:24 PM||||The clutch  in my car began to stop disengaging,so I took it all apart and found a broken disc.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ed||09-30-2000||01:39 PM||||My '87 GT's clutch starting slipping at about 112,000 miles.  The rear engine cam seal had failed and the disc got oiled.<P>Solution was a complete clutch job.  <P>Incidentally, the original clutch brand (LUK) seems adequate for my driving.  We looked at the disc and there was still an adequate mount of facing left.  Other types are better for really hard driving or V-8's, but OEM seems okay to me for moderate driving.  (And I don't mean just driving around town, but some galloping out in the hills too)||||reg||1
